Subject: [PATCH] vo_mediacodec: leave the OSD pipeline asleep while nothing can change

vo_mediacodec files an OSD request for every video frame. The render
thread wakes, serves it from the pre-render of the frame before, asks
how far the subtitle decoders can render, renders the next frame ahead
with osd_render() and looks for an event to warm; the flip then
publishes the frame's release on the pipeline's condition, which wakes
the render and the present thread once more. With no subtitle track and
nothing on the OSD, all of it finds 24 times a second that nothing
changed: on a file without subtitles the two threads make 62 wakeups a
second for 3.5 Mcycles/s on a Pixel 7 and 5.1 on a SEI box.

Without a subtitle decoder, what the OSD shows only changes when
something tells osd_state so: text, the bar, an overlay, an option.
osd_state now counts those changes (osd_note_change), and
osd_get_static_id() gives a VO a value that stays the same exactly
while every pts renders the same, 0 while a subtitle decoder is
attached. vo_mediacodec combines it with the OSD window's size and
sends it along with the frame's request. Once a render made for a
request with that id is queued or on screen, osd_ahead holds the id
settled (osd_ahead_is_settled), and vo_mediacodec files no request and
publishes no release until the id, the window's size or the epoch moves:
an OSD change, a subtitle track, a seek, a pause, a new geometry and a
repaint each move one of them. Only a render on demand settles an id: a
pre-render may be older than the change the id stands for, a result
that was not queued leaves a repaint owed, and a static OSD is not
rendered ahead at all. With a subtitle decoder attached every frame is
requested, pre-rendered and prefetched as before, and so is a plane
presented on every frame (--vo-mediacodec-osd-vsync-delay): for those
two the VO passes no id and does not ask the window for its size. Up to
API 30 a SurfaceView's buffer queue lives in SurfaceFlinger, and
ANativeWindow_getWidth() and _getHeight() are a binder call each: asked
for every frame they cost the VO thread 42 to 63 context switches a
second and half again its CPU time on a SHIELD (API 28) and a Fire TV
Stick 4K (API 30), which with subtitles on, where there is nothing to
save, made playback dearer than before (SHIELD: 12.1 -> 12.4 % of a
core). While the OSD is static the VO still asks, where the render
thread did before: that is how a resized window is noticed.

H.264 1080p without subtitles, Tortuga's options, interleaved, n = 5,
median (range), app cycles and wakeups:
  Pixel 7      197 (194-200) -> 189 (188-189) Mcycles/s, 735 -> 673 /s
  SEI Box R    383 (381-387) -> 377 (377-382) Mcycles/s, 750 -> 688 /s
vo/osd-render and vo/osd-present leave the per-thread tables on both.
A scripted playback on the Pixel (no track; show-text at 6 s for 3 s;
sid=1 at 12 s; sid=no at 22 s) files 252 requests instead of 687 and
presents the text, its removal and every cue at the same pts as the
unpatched build (17 against 16 swaps); screenshots show the text, a cue
and the empty plane.

